-- title:
--   Nodal completion of the stalk after coefficient change
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and $M$ with $p \mid M$, $p^2 \nmid M$, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit whose image under $\mathbb{Z}/M \to \mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$ is $1$, and the hypothesis $hj$ that $jqModC\,\mathbb{Q}$ lies in the $q$-expansion function field of $SL(2,\mathbb{Z})$; let $\mathfrak{X}$ be an `XHDRModelAtP` datum for level $\Gamma_M(M,H)$ over $R\,p$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a non-unit, with algebraically closed residue field of characteristic $p$, and $\rho : R\,p \to A$ inducing the structure map to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Let $O$ be a discrete valuation domain with $\rho_O : R\,p \to O$, maximal ideal generated by $p$, together with compatible maps $toκ : O \to \kappa(A)$, $jO : O \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and $\iota_A : O \to A$ (so $\iota_A$ induces $jO$ and $toκ$). Let $n$ be a point of the fibre product of the two branch morphisms $\mathfrak{X}.comp\,A\,hA\,\rho\,h\rho\,0$ and $\mathfrak{X}.comp\,\ldots\,1$, and write $x_n$ for its image in $XO(\Gamma_M(M,H),hj,\rho_O)$ under the first projection followed by branch $0$ and `bcMap`. The crossing chart data consist of $e \ge 1$, an open $U$ of $XO(\ldots,\rho_O)$ containing $x_n$, and $f : U \to \mathrm{Spec}\bigl(O[X_0,X_1]/(X_0X_1-p^e)\bigr)$ such that: $f$ is a morphism over $\mathrm{Spec}\,O$ (hypothesis `hover`); both coordinates $U,V$ lie in the prime $f(y)$ exactly when $y$ lies over $x_n$; at every such $y$ the stalk map of $f$ is flat, carries the maximal ideal onto the maximal ideal, and induces an isomorphism of residue fields; every such $y$ has a neighbourhood on which $f$ is étale; and the two branches match the two coordinates, in that $V \in f(y)$ holds precisely when $y$ lies in the image of branch $0$ and $U \in f(y)$ precisely when $y$ lies in the image of branch $1$. Finally let $O'$ be a second discrete valuation domain with $\sigma : O \to O'$ and an injective local $\iota_{A'} : O' \to A$ with $\iota_{A'} \circ \sigma = \iota_A$, together with $jO' : O' \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and the compatibilities $\iota_{A'}$ induces $jO'$, $jO' \circ \sigma \circ \rho_O$ is the structure map, and $htoκ'$. The statement introduces abbreviations: the geometric generic fibre $XQ$ with its maps $prJ$, $prJ'$ to the two integral models and the open $VM$ on $\mathfrak{X}.Meta.C$ (these four do not occur in the conclusion); the sections $gu, gv$ of $XO(\ldots,\rho_O)$ over $U.\iota\,''\,\top$ obtained from the coordinates $U,V$ of the crossing ring via $f$ on global sections; the base-change map $bc'$ for $\sigma \circ \rho_O$, the point $x_n'$ of $XO(\ldots,\sigma \circ \rho_O)$ defined as $x_n$ but through $bc'$, the projection $pr_\sigma : XO(\ldots,\sigma \circ \rho_O) \to XO(\ldots,\rho_O)$ over $\sigma$, the stalk $B$ at $x_n'$, and the ring map $\sigma_B : O' \to B$ germ of the structure map. The conclusion asserts, for every generator $\varpi'$ of the maximal ideal of $O'$: $B$ is a Noetherian domain, and there exist $E' \ge 1$, an $\mathfrak{m}$-adically complete discrete valuation domain $W'$, a map $\sigma' : O' \to W'$ with maximal ideal of $W'$ generated by $\sigma'\varpi'$ and with $O' \to \kappa(W')$ surjective, and a ring isomorphism $\iota' : \widehat{B} \to \mathrm{MvPowerSeries}(\mathrm{Fin}\,2, W')/\mathrm{uvCrossingIdeal}(W', (\sigma'\varpi')^{E'})$ from the adic completion of $B$ at its maximal ideal, such that $\iota'$ sends the image of $\sigma_B(o)$ to the constant $\sigma'(o)$ for all $o \in O'$, and, $x_n'$ lying in $pr_\sigma^{-1}(U.\iota\,''\,\top)$, $\iota'$ sends the completed germ at $x_n'$ of $pr_\sigma^{*}gu$ to $U$ times a unit and the completed germ of $pr_\sigma^{*}gv$ to $V$.
--
--   This is the Deligne–Rapoport description of the local ring of the integral model of $X_H(M)$ at a node of the fibre at $p$, transported to a possibly ramified second coefficient ring $O'$: the completed stalk is $W'\llbracket U,V\rrbracket/(UV-\pi^{E'})$, with the two coordinates adapted to the two branches supplied by the étale crossing chart. It is used downstream to read sections of the model at the node and to compute their orders along the branches.
